    "name": "Dimension Stone",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/mining/mineral-resources/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Dimension stone is natural rock extracted as blocks or slabs to meet architectural or engineering specifications. Iranian quarry studies show how fractures, block geometry, material properties, and operating risks influence the amount of usable stone a deposit can supply.",
    "tags": ["Dimension stone", "Quarrying", "Natural stone", "Isfahan", "Rock engineering"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T11:36:10Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T11:36:10Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Dimension Stone in Iran: Quarries and Usable Blocks | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iranian dimension-stone resources through quarry research on fractures, recoverable blocks, material testing, and operational risks."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "stone-as-a-resource",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A resource defined by usable shape",
        "content": "Dimension stone is rock quarried to obtain blocks or slabs with specified dimensions and shapes. Architectural selection also considers color, grain, pattern, surface finish, strength, durability, and the ability to take a polish. The defining question is therefore more demanding than whether solid rock exists at a site: can it supply material of the required form and quality? [s1]\n\nThat distinction makes the quarry itself central to the resource story. A rock mass may contain attractive stone yet be divided by natural fractures into pieces that do not match a customer's requirements. An Iranian study at the Lashotor quarry complex in Isfahan Province examined exactly this problem, investigating an abandoned bench where waste had discouraged further extraction. A bench is a working step or level within a quarry. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "fractures-and-recovery",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the Lashotor investigation demonstrates",
        "content": "The Lashotor researchers mapped discontinuities—the surfaces separating parts of the rock mass—and modeled the blocks between them. Their approach assessed block shape as well as dimensions against a target market. The analysis identified potentially marketable blocks within a bench that had been left unused. [s2]\n\nThe finding supports a specific conclusion: geological characterization can change an extraction decision. It does not establish that all abandoned Iranian quarries contain profitable stone, or that the modeled blocks were subsequently sold. Its wider relevance lies in the sequence of questions: identify fractures, determine the resulting blocks, and evaluate those blocks against an actual product requirement before choosing a cutting pattern. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "properties-and-place",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Regional examples require material-specific evidence",
        "content": "Research on carbonate rocks collected from Iranian quarries distinguishes travertines from limestones and describes differences in visible cavities, texture, and appearance. The investigators prepared specimens and tested their engineering properties under controlled conditions. Such work demonstrates why a decorative description cannot substitute for a measured material property. A polished surface tells a viewer about appearance; it does not independently establish strength. [s3]\n\nThese laboratory samples also have a limited geographic meaning. They represent the selected rocks and test conditions, rather than every quarry selling a similarly named stone. For international readers, the useful distinction is between a regional example, a commercial description, and a tested specimen. Treating those as interchangeable would conceal the variation that makes natural stone both attractive and technically demanding. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "risk-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Beyond the rock face",
        "content": "A separate study examined the Ghasre Dasht marble quarry in northeastern Fars Province through an expert-based risk-assessment method. In that case, economic, management, and schedule risks ranked prominently. These were results of a particular decision model applied to a particular quarry, rather than measured national accident rates or a universal ranking of hazards. [s4]\n\nTogether, the Iranian cases show that a stone resource becomes useful through several kinds of evidence: geological information about intact blocks, tests of the material, and an assessment of how extraction will be organized. They also explain why a national production chart would be misleading without a documented statistical series. The inspected USGS compilation explicitly identifies insufficient information for a reliable dimension-stone output estimate. This page consequently uses documented cases rather than presenting an unsupported national total. [s1][s2][s3][s4][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
